## Enums in Java – The “Type‑Safe” Enum

Java’s `enum` type was added in JDK 5.0.  
It lets you create a **named set of constant values** that behaves like a class.

```java
public enum Day {
    MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY,
    THURSDAY, FRIDAY, SATURDAY, SUNDAY
}
```

### Why use an enum?

| Problem | Traditional solution | Enum solution |
|---------|----------------------|---------------|
| **Fixed set of constants** | `public static final int` fields (or `String`s) | Named, type‑safe constants |
| **Human‑readable** | Often use `String` or `int` codes | Directly see the constant name |
| **Prevent invalid values** | Developer might use an arbitrary `int` | Compile‑time check – only enum values allowed |
| **Add behavior** | Separate static methods | Methods, fields, and constructors can live inside the enum |
| **Iterate over values** | Keep an array of constants | `Day.values()` returns an array of all constants |

---

## Defining an enum

```java
public enum Day {
    // 1️⃣ Constants (they are public static final instances of Day)
    MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY,
    FRIDAY, SATURDAY, SUNDAY;

    // 2️⃣ Optional fields
    private final boolean isWeekend;

    // 3️⃣ Constructor (runs for each constant)
    Day() {
        this.isWeekend = (this == SATURDAY || this == SUNDAY);
    }

    // 4️⃣ Instance method
    public boolean isWeekend() {
        return isWeekend;
    }

    // 5️⃣ Override toString if you want a nicer display
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name();   // default is fine, but you could use a map or formatting
    }
}
```

> **Key points**
> - Enum constants (`MONDAY`, …) are created automatically.
> - They are `public static final` – you never write `new Day()`.
> - An enum *is* a class, so you can give it fields, constructors, and methods.
> - The compiler enforces that only the declared constants can exist.

---

## Using an enum

```java
public class EnumDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1️⃣ Assign an enum value
        Day today = Day.FRIDAY;

        // 2️⃣ Use in a switch (Java 14+ also supports arrow syntax)
        switch (today) {
            case MONDAY -> System.out.println("Start of work week");
            case FRIDAY -> System.out.println("Almost weekend");
            case SATURDAY, SUNDAY -> System.out.println("Weekend!");
            default -> System.out.println("Midweek day");
        }

        // 3️⃣ Call an instance method
        System.out.println(today + " is weekend? " + today.isWeekend());

        // 4️⃣ Iterate over all constants
        for (Day d : Day.values()) {
            System.out.println(d + " => " + (d.isWeekend() ? "yes" : "no"));
        }

        // 5️⃣ Convert from String (handle invalid input)
        try {
            Day fromString = Day.valueOf("MONDAY");
            System.out.println("Parsed: " + fromString);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid day string");
        }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
Almost weekend
FRIDAY is weekend? false
MONDAY => no
TUESDAY => no
WEDNESDAY => no
THURSDAY => no
FRIDAY => no
SATURDAY => yes
SUNDAY => yes
Parsed: MONDAY
```

